- Household income and consumption outcomes.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Households' Income and Consumption Expenditure in 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China","publication_date":"2023-01-18","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202301/t20230118_1892303.html","claim":"The NBS reported that real per-capita disposable income rose 2.9 percent in 2022, rural income grew faster than urban income, and real per-capita consumption fell 0.2 percent, with the median income still below the mean.","locator":"lines 21-24 and 29-32","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary household survey release with detailed distributional measures, though still official data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022 household outcomes published immediately after year end.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level household outcomes are only partially attributable to Xi, but they are the best official evidence of household welfare under his 2022 rule.","contrary_evidence":["Urban households' real income growth was only 1.9 percent and real consumption fell.","The release does not isolate ruler-specific causality from pandemic and macro conditions."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- R&D and technology investment expanded.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 National R&D Expenditure Statistics Bulletin","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China / Ministry of Science and Technology / Ministry of Finance","publication_date":"2023-09-18","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/sj/zxfb/202309/t20230918_1942920.html","claim":"China's 2022 R&D spending reached 30,782.9 billion yuan, up 10.1 percent year on year, with intensity at 2.54 percent of GDP and basic research growing 11.4 percent.","locator":"lines 623-630 and 636","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary statistical bulletin with a clear full-year investment measure, though subject to official-data caveats.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022 report published later in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects Xi-era priorities for technology and innovation, though the R&D system is also shaped by ministries, firms, and provinces.","contrary_evidence":["Higher R&D spending does not guarantee efficient allocation or broad prosperity.","It does not by itself prove that innovation policy improved household welfare in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}

- Financial-sector supervision and anti-corruption claim.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press conference on reform and development of China's financial sector since the 18th CPC National Congress","publisher":"english.scio.gov.cn","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://english.scio.gov.cn/m/pressroom/2022-06/29/content_78296373_13.htm","claim":"Officials said financial supervision had been tightened, more than 124 stockholders who breached laws or regulations had been disclosed, and corruption behind financial risks had been investigated and punished.","locator":"press-conference summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official account with concrete enforcement figures, but it is still a self-justifying report from the sector it describes.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 press conference summarizing the Xi era and ongoing 2022 reform posture.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Xi-era party-state financial governance model, though specific cases were handled by regulators and discipline bodies.","contrary_evidence":["This is an official self-report and may overstate effectiveness.","A crackdown can reflect political discipline as well as genuine anti-corruption."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.3","5B.7"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across official Chinese policy releases, NBS statistical releases, the PBOC, the IMF, the World Bank, Reuters, and OECD-style structural commentary. I kept 16 non-duplicative URLs that collectively cover macro stabilization, monetary easing, housing rescue, opening-up, small-business support, data-policy design, COVID policy correction, leadership composition, household outcomes, R&D, and anti-corruption.

I rejected or de-prioritized:
- Duplicate Reuters syndications and mirrors of the same underlying story, especially on the leadership reshuffle, property rescue, and COVID rollback.
- Out-of-period follow-ups from 2023-2025 that were useful only as retrospectives but did not add target-year 2022 ruler-period detail.
- Broad China-economy commentary without a clear 2022 ruler-attributed policy claim.
- Background pieces on SOEs, competition, or productivity that repeated the same structural critique already covered by the IMF and World Bank.
- Structured dataset re-fetches already covered by the local priors, including WDI-style growth, income, and spending series.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of Xi personally choosing specific macro instruments is still limited; many concrete 2022 measures are formally State Council, PBOC, NDRC, or regulator actions within a Xi-led system.
- Distributional evidence is still mostly household averages and rural-vs-urban splits; I do not yet have strong independent evidence on wealth concentration, Gini movement, or regional divergence beyond those proxies.
- The evidence on appointments is strong for loyalist consolidation, but weaker on whether any economic technocrats retained meaningful autonomy inside the 2022 system.
- Anti-corruption and favoritism evidence is still mostly official self-reporting; more independent work on finance, SOEs, and local patronage would help.
- If you want, the next useful search would be on independent business-confidence surveys, FDI flows, and 2022 property-sector distress transmission to households and local government finance.

