- Education policy prioritized equity, funding, and teacher pay.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 education work priorities","publisher":"Ministry of Education of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-02-08","url":"https://www.moe.gov.cn/jyb_xwfb/gzdt_gzdt/202202/t20220208_597666.html","claim":"The Ministry of Education said 2022 priorities included implementing the 14th Five-Year education plan, strengthening education as a priority in public finance with no less than 4% of GDP, protecting teachers' pay, improving balanced compulsory education, and monitoring implementation.","locator":"sections 5, 6, 15, and 25 of the article text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry policy text with concrete targets and implementation themes, but it is still official self-description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 priorities and implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Xi-era education governance rather than Xi alone; useful for assessing service delivery intent and institutional management.","contrary_evidence":["An announced priority is not the same as realized access or quality.","Some of the language is aspirational and party-orthodox."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Compulsory education was already broadly universal and more balanced across regions.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"推动义务教育走向更加优质均衡","publisher":"Ministry of Education of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-07-01","url":"https://www.moe.gov.cn/jyb_xwfb/s5148/202207/t20220701_642351.html","claim":"The Education Ministry said China had achieved county-level basic balanced development of compulsory education across all 2,895 counties, rural schools had improved markedly, and the nine-year compulsory education retention rate had reached 95.4%.","locator":"article body discussing the nationwide balanced-development and dropout-clearing achievements","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry article with concrete nationwide attainment figures, though celebratory and not independent.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 policy/outcome summary.","ruler_attribution":"Broad national education-system evidence under Xi-era governance; not exclusive personal attribution, but directly relevant to ruler-period performance.","contrary_evidence":["The source is official and promotional.","It does not isolate learning quality or subnational inequality that persists beneath national averages."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Zero-COVID caused major suffering and blocked access to care and necessities.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: China","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/china","claim":"HRW reported that China's strict zero-COVID policy kept residents confined for long periods, impeded access to health care, food, and other necessities, and that an unknown number of people died after being denied medical treatment for non-COVID illnesses.","locator":"lines 123-125","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"High-quality independent human-rights monitoring with concrete examples and clear period fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly describes 2022 lockdown practice and its consequences, published immediately after the target year.","ruler_attribution":"The policy was maintained under Xi's leadership, so the harms are materially attributable to Xi-era governance, even if many measures were implemented by local officials.","contrary_evidence":["HRW also notes the policy prevented some Covid deaths and illnesses.","The report does not quantify the full national death toll from non-Covid deprivation."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Late-2022 protests reflected widespread distress over restrictions and censorship.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China: Unprecedented Nationwide Protests Against Abuses","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/01/12/china-unprecedented-nationwide-protests-against-abuses","claim":"HRW said people across China peacefully protested draconian Covid-19 restrictions in late 2022, while authorities had repeatedly imposed unpredictable lockdowns on hundreds of millions of people, impeding access to health care, food, and necessities.","locator":"lines 615-616 and 620-622","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent NGO release with specific period-linked claims and clear attribution to the central policy environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 events reported in January 2023.","ruler_attribution":"The nationwide pattern is attributable to Xi-era Covid governance, though this source does not prove Xi personally ordered each local restriction.","contrary_evidence":["The protests show civilian pressure and policy responsiveness, not just repression.","This source is partly overlapping with HRW's World Report chapter."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The State Council pivoted away from one-size-fits-all Covid controls.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19 response further optimized with 10 new measures","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/statecouncil/ministries/202212/08/content_WS63913c92c6d0a757729e4135.html","claim":"China announced 10 new Covid measures that told localities to rectify one-size-fits-all excesses, prohibit arbitrary expansion of high-risk areas, reduce mass testing, allow home quarantine for eligible cases, and keep basic medical services and pharmacies functioning.","locator":"lines 45-63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary policy circular with explicit operational corrections and service-protection instructions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022 policy correction, issued in December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Xi-era top-level policy reversal through the State Council and joint prevention mechanism; strong ruler-period evidence, but not Xi-alone authorship.","contrary_evidence":["The pivot came after major social and economic harm.","Implementation followed severe earlier lockdown damage, so this is corrective evidence rather than proof of earlier restraint."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}

- End-of-year official statistics show weak consumption and stressed labor markets despite growth.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Economy Withstood Pressure and Reached a New Level in 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China","publication_date":"2023-01-17","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202301/t20230117_1892094.html?from=%25252525252525252540","claim":"The NBS reported 3.0 percent GDP growth in 2022, 5.1 percent growth in fixed-asset investment, a 10.0 percent fall in real-estate development investment, 10.5 percent export growth, and a December youth unemployment rate of 16.7 percent.","locator":"lines 52-54 and 68-81","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary statistical release with the standard full-year national outcome series, though official data still deserve caution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022 outcome release published immediately after year end.","ruler_attribution":"Country outcomes under Xi-era rule, not automatic personal credit or blame, but the data are the main end-of-year welfare backdrop.","contrary_evidence":["Official statistics may understate distress in private firms and local labor markets.","National averages can hide severe household and regional strain."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}

- Household incomes rose, but consumption was weak and the median lagged the mean.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Households' Income and Consumption Expenditure in 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China","publication_date":"2023-01-18","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202301/t20230118_1892303.html","claim":"The NBS said real per-capita disposable income rose 2.9 percent in 2022, rural income grew faster than urban income, consumption fell 0.2 percent in real terms, and the national median income was 85.1 percent of the average.","locator":"lines 21-32","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary household-survey release with useful distributional detail, though still an official data source.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022 household outcomes published immediately after year end.","ruler_attribution":"This is country-level welfare outcome evidence under Xi-era rule; attribution is partial but materially relevant.","contrary_evidence":["Urban real income growth was much weaker than rural growth.","The release does not isolate the effects of Xi's choices from pandemic and macro shocks."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 distinct documents across official Chinese policy and statistical releases, IMF and World Bank macro surveillance, UNICEF data, and human-rights monitoring.

I retained the smallest non-duplicative set that covered:
- welfare intent and baseline social provision,
- medical and pension/social-security coverage,
- education access and equalization,
- crisis correction under Covid,
- household and labor-market outcomes,
- and contrary evidence on avoidable suffering from zero-Covid.

I rejected or de-prioritized:
- duplicate mirrors and syndications of the same policy story,
- broad China-economy commentary that repeated the IMF or World Bank without new 2022 ruler-period detail,
- 2023-2025 follow-ups that were useful only as retrospectives,
- sources that were too generic, too much like party rhetoric, or too far from the 2022 target period,
- and dataset re-fetches already represented by the local priors.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct Xi-personal attribution is still limited for most social policy. Much of the concrete action is State Council, ministry, or party-state system evidence rather than a signed Xi order.
- Education and health access are better evidenced than housing quality, disability services, and early-childhood outcomes.
- I have decent evidence on national averages, but weaker evidence on subnational inequality, quality of service, and the lived experience of migrants, disabled people, and minority regions in 2022.
- The social-protection picture is strong on coverage, weaker on adequacy and benefit value.
- If you want the dossier tightened further, the next useful search would be on disability services, migrant welfare access, and independent evaluation of local delivery in health and education.

chapter_notes.7B:
**Reused Evidence**
No prior evidence items genuinely applied to 7B, so there are no `REUSE_JSON` lines.

**New Evidence**
The dossier is anchored in a mix of contemporaneous reporting on elite selection, official anti-corruption and family-conflict rules, data-fraud enforcement, and one historical investigation on hidden family wealth. The strongest 2022 evidence is about loyalty-based appointments and controlled scrutiny, not direct proof of personal enrichment.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"How Xi Rewrote China’s Rulebook to Build the Party Around Himself","publisher":"Bloomberg","publication_date":"2022-10-11","url":"https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2022-china-national-party-congress-xi-rules/","claim":"Bloomberg’s analysis says Xi consolidated power by rewriting party rules, breaking succession norms, and relying on a decade-long corruption crackdown that remade the party leadership around his own allies.","locator":"analysis text and section on purges and power plays","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Substantive contemporaneous analysis drawing on official sources and appointment data; strong for elite-control pattern, though still journalistic synthesis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 congress and the immediate run-up.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi because it concerns his rule, his rule changes, and his selected elite network.","contrary_evidence":["The article is analytical rather than a single documentary finding.","It does not by itself prove personal enrichment or private self-dealing."],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.8","7B.10"]}

